Two factor authentication (2FA) and multi factor authentication (MFA) are security procedures and mechanisms that are designed to reduce the risk of unauthorised account 
access. Traditional password only authentication relies on a single piece of information that can be stolen, guessed or obtained through other ways like phishing and social engineering attacks. MFA adds an additional layer of security and authentication f
actor. Meaning that just possessing the password of an account shouldn't be enough to access an account and the information on it. The National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) defines three traditional authentication factor categories, }{
