\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \lang2057\langfe1033\langfenp1033\insrsid6513979\charrsid983060 The first category, \'93something you know\'94
, refers to information that is known by the user, such as a PIN or password. Passwords are widely used but can be vulnerable to phishing attacks, password reuse, credential stuffing and data breaches.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 
\insrsid10976062\charrsid983060 
\par 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \lang2057\langfe1033\langfenp1033\insrsid6513979\charrsid983060 The second category, \'93something you have\'94
, refers to an item or device controlled by the user, such as a mobile phone, authenticator app, or a physical security key.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid10976062\charrsid983060 
\par 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \lang2057\langfe1033\langfenp1033\insrsid6513979\charrsid983060 The third category, \'93something you are\'94
, refers to biometric characteristics, such as fingerprints, facial recognition technology and retinal scans. NIST explains that MFA involves using more than one of these different factor categories }{\field{\*\fldinst {\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 
