\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \lang2057\langfe1033\langfenp1033\insrsid6513979\charrsid983060 Common MFA methods usually include SMS codes, authenticator applications, and hardware security keys.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 
\insrsid10976062\charrsid983060 
\par 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \lang2057\langfe1033\langfenp1033\insrsid6513979\charrsid983060 SMS authentication sends a }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \lang2057\langfe1033\langfenp1033\insrsid983060\charrsid983060 one-time}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 
\lang2057\langfe1033\langfenp1033\insrsid6513979\charrsid983060 
 code to the user's registered mobile phone number, that is valid for a limited amount of time before being deleted and unusable. Although convenient and widely supported. SMS still does have security weaknesses. Attackers can potentially take control of 
a victim's mobile phone number through SIM swapping and some weaknesses in telecommunication infrastructure can also expose SMS authentication codes and be intercepted and used. NIST therefore classifies the use of SMS and voice calls through the public t
elephone network as a restricted authentication method because attackers have demonstrated the ability to obtain reassigned telephone numbers and intercept authentication messages }{\field{\*\fldinst {\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 
